{s:2,k:'bookfig',term:"Figure: the entropy account of mixing",title:"Mixing hot and cold water",view:[0.0, 10.0, 720.0, 340.0],say:'Now the mixing example. Two kilograms at three hundred fifty-three kelvin, three at two ninety-three. They meet at three hundred seventeen. Below is the entropy account. The hot water loses, the cold water gains a little more, and the leftover is S gen. Nature always keeps a small tip.',cues:{s1:'Two kilograms at',s2:'They meet at',s3:'Below is the',s4:'and the leftover',cap:'the cold water'},steps:4,cap:'The cold water gains more than the hot water loses: Sgen > 0.',fig:{"vb": [0.0, 0.0, 720.0, 360.0], "defs": "<defs><marker id=\"ar-f3-8-1-n\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" /></marker><marker id=\"ar-f3-8-1-m\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" style=\"fill:var(--line2)\" /></marker><marker id=\"ar-f3-8-1-l\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" style=\"fill:var(--live)\" /></marker><marker id=\"ar-f3-8-1-x\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" style=\"fill:var(--next)\" /></marker></defs>", "items": [[360.0, "<text x=\"360.0\" y=\"26.0\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" font-weight=\"700\">Temperatures, K</text>", 1], [90.0, "<line x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"650.0\" y2=\"70.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [127.3, "<line x1=\"127.3\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"127.3\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [127.3, "<text x=\"127.3\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">290</text>", 1], [202.0, "<line x1=\"202.0\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"202.0\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [202.0, "<text x=\"202.0\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">300</text>", 1], [276.7, "<line x1=\"276.7\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"276.7\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [276.7, "<text x=\"276.7\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">310</text>", 1], [351.3, "<line x1=\"351.3\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"351.3\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [351.3, "<text x=\"351.3\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">320</text>", 1], [426.0, "<line x1=\"426.0\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"426.0\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [426.0, "<text x=\"426.0\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">330</text>", 1], [500.7, "<line x1=\"500.7\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"500.7\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [500.7, "<text x=\"500.7\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">340</text>", 1], [575.3, "<line x1=\"575.3\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"575.3\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [575.3, "<text x=\"575.3\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">350</text>", 1], [650.0, "<line x1=\"650.0\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"650.0\" y2=\"75.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line2);stroke-width:1.5\" />", 1], [650.0, "<text x=\"650.0\" y=\"90.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">360</text>", 1], [597.7, "<circle cx=\"597.7\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"7\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--fig);stroke-width:2\" />", 1], [597.7, "<text x=\"597.7\" y=\"58.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" font-weight=\"700\">2 kg at 353 K</text>", 1], [149.7, "<circle cx=\"149.7\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"7\" style=\"fill:var(--live);stroke:var(--fig);stroke-width:2\" />", 1], [149.7, "<text x=\"149.7\" y=\"58.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--live)\" font-weight=\"700\">3 kg at 293 K</text>", 1], [328.9, "<circle cx=\"328.9\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"7\" style=\"fill:var(--next);stroke:var(--fig);stroke-width:2\" />", 2], [328.9, "<text x=\"328.9\" y=\"58.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--next)\" font-weight=\"700\">mix 317 K</text>", 2], [575.3, "<line x1=\"575.3\" y1=\"106.0\" x2=\"351.3\" y2=\"106.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#ar-f3-8-1-n)\" />", 2], [172.1, "<line x1=\"172.1\" y1=\"106.0\" x2=\"306.5\" y2=\"106.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--live);stroke-width:2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#ar-f3-8-1-l)\" />", 2], [463.3, "<text x=\"463.3\" y=\"128.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\">hot water cools</text>", 2], [239.3, "<text x=\"239.3\" y=\"128.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--live)\">cold warms</text>", 2], [360.0, "<text x=\"360.0\" y=\"186.0\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" font-weight=\"700\">Entropy changes, kJ/K</text>", 3], [360.0, "<line x1=\"360.0\" y1=\"198.0\" x2=\"360.0\" y2=\"320.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.5;stroke-dasharray:4 4\" />", 3], [89.8, "<rect x=\"89.8\" y=\"206.0\" width=\"270.2\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--accent-soft);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2\" />", 3], [352.0, "<text x=\"352.0\" y=\"224.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" font-weight=\"700\">hot water −0.9008</text>", 3], [360.0, "<rect x=\"360.0\" y=\"242.0\" width=\"296.7\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--live-soft);stroke:var(--live);stroke-width:2\" />", 3], [368.0, "<text x=\"368.0\" y=\"260.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" font-weight=\"700\">cold water +0.9889</text>", 3], [360.0, "<rect x=\"360.0\" y=\"282.0\" width=\"26.5\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--next-soft);stroke:var(--next);stroke-width:2\" />", 4], [394.5, "<text x=\"394.5\" y=\"300.0\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--next)\" font-weight=\"700\">sum = S<tspan dy=\"4\" font-size=\"11\">gen</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> </tspan> = +0.0882 &gt; 0</text>", 4], [360.0, "<text x=\"360.0\" y=\"342.0\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\">0</text>", 3]]}},
{s:2,k:'notebook',term:'Key ideas',title:'The principle of increase of entropy',say:'Write the full account: the system change plus the surroundings change equals S gen, never negative. Seal both in one box, and that isolated system only gains entropy. Heat crossing a temperature gap is the commonest source. And a reversible adiabatic process generates none: it is isentropic.',cues:{i1:'Write the full',i2:'Seal both in',i3:'Heat crossing a',i4:'And a reversible'},items:[['ΔS system + ΔS surroundings = Sgen ≥ 0','i1'],['Isolated system: entropy rises, never falls','i2'],['Heat across a gap: Sgen = Q(1/Tcold − 1/Thot)','i3'],['Reversible adiabatic: Sgen = 0, ΔS = 0','i4']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{
 const y=[8,180,352,524];
 /* i1: a system inside its surroundings, the two changes adding up */
 const r1=TH_box(14,y[0],612,160,{st:BLU,sw:3})+`<rect x="34" y="${y[0]+18}" width="250" height="124" rx="10" fill="#EEF4FF" stroke="${TH_GREY}" stroke-width="2"/>`+
  `<rect x="74" y="${y[0]+46}" width="130" height="70" rx="8" fill="#FFE8CC" stroke="${BLU}" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="8 6"/>`+TH_T(139,y[0]+88,'system',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+
  TH_T(270,y[0]+136,'surroundings',{fs:20,fw:700,a:'end',fill:TH_GREY})+
  TH_T(306,y[0]+62,`ΔS${'<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="70%">sys</tspan>'} + ΔS${'<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="70%">surr</tspan>'}`,{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU})+TH_T(306,y[0]+104,`= S${'<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="70%">gen</tspan>'} ≥ 0`,{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU})+TH_T(306,y[0]+138,'zero only if reversible',{fs:22,fill:TH_GREY});
 /* i2: the isolated box: system and surroundings sealed together */
 const r2=TH_box(14,y[1],612,160,{st:ORG,sw:3})+`<rect x="40" y="${y[1]+22}" width="230" height="116" rx="6" fill="#FFF4E6" stroke="${TH_DARK}" stroke-width="8"/><rect x="52" y="${y[1]+34}" width="206" height="92" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="${TH_DARK}" stroke-width="2"/>`+
  TH_T(155,y[1]+70,'system +',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:TH_DARK})+TH_T(155,y[1]+100,'surroundings',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:TH_DARK})+
  TH_arrow(320,y[1]+132,320,y[1]+30,ORG,6,20)+TH_T(350,y[1]+62,'isolated: S rises',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG})+TH_T(350,y[1]+96,'in every real process,',{fs:22,fill:TH_GREY})+TH_T(350,y[1]+126,'never falls',{fs:22,fill:TH_GREY});
 /* i3: 50 kJ from 400 K to 300 K, computed Sgen = 0.0417 kJ/K */
 const r3=TH_box(14,y[2],612,160,{st:PUR,sw:3})+`<rect x="40" y="${y[2]+24}" width="130" height="76" rx="8" fill="${TH_HOT}"/>`+TH_T(105,y[2]+70,'400 K',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:'#FFFFFF'})+
  `<rect x="440" y="${y[2]+24}" width="130" height="76" rx="8" fill="${TH_COLD}"/>`+TH_T(505,y[2]+70,'300 K',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:'#FFFFFF'})+
  TH_arrow(184,y[2]+62,426,y[2]+62,PUR,6,20)+TH_T(305,y[2]+48,'Q = 50 kJ',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:PUR})+
  TH_T(320,y[2]+140,'Sgen = 50(1/300 − 1/400) = 0.0417 kJ/K',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:PUR});
 /* i4: a reversible adiabatic turbine: a vertical line on T-s */
 const b4=y[3]+144;
 const r4=TH_box(14,y[3],612,160,{st:GRN,sw:3})+`<path d="M60,${y[3]+60} L150,${y[3]+30} L150,${y[3]+130} L60,${y[3]+100} Z" fill="${TH_LIGHT}" stroke="${TH_STEEL}" stroke-width="4"/>`+TH_T(105,y[3]+86,'turbine',{fs:20,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TH_DARK})+
  TH_ax(200,b4,190,128)+TH_L(300,y[3]+40,300,y[3]+122,GRN,6)+`<circle cx="300" cy="${y[3]+40}" r="7" fill="${TH_DARK}"/><circle cx="300" cy="${y[3]+122}" r="7" fill="${TH_DARK}"/>`+
  TH_T(314,y[3]+48,'1',{fs:22,fw:800})+TH_T(314,y[3]+128,'2',{fs:22,fw:800})+
  TH_T(410,y[3]+60,'Q = 0, no friction',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN})+TH_T(410,y[3]+94,'Sgen = 0, ΔS = 0',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN})+TH_T(410,y[3]+126,'isentropic',{fs:22,fill:TH_GREY});
 return g('pop',tk(c,'i1'),r1)+g('pop',tk(c,'i2'),r2)+g('pop',tk(c,'i3'),r3)+g('pop',tk(c,'i4'),r4)}},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2",title:"Worked example 2: mixing water",say:'First, kelvin: three hundred fifty-three and two hundred ninety-three. The vessel is insulated, so the heat lost equals the heat gained, and the mix settles at three hundred seventeen kelvin, forty-four degrees Celsius. The hot water loses entropy: minus zero point nine zero zero eight.',cues:{fk:'First, kelvin',k1:'three hundred fifty-three',k1b:'two hundred ninety-three',k2:'The vessel is',k2b:'the mix settles',k3:'The hot water',k3b:'minus zero point'},q:['Insulated vessel: 2 kg of water at 80 °C mixed with 3 kg at 20 °C.','cp = 4.187 kJ/kg·K. Find Tf and the total ΔS. Is the mixing reversible?'],f:['Tf = (m₁T₁ + m₂T₂) / (m₁ + m₂)','ΔS = m cp ln(Tf / T) for each mass','Sgen = ΔS₁ + ΔS₂ (insulated)'],hint:'Kelvin first. No heat or work crosses the vessel wall.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Kelvin", ["80 + 273 and 20 + 273","T₁ = 353 K, T₂ = 293 K"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Final temperature", ["(706 + 879) / 5 = 1585 / 5","Tf = 317 K = 44 °C"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Hot water", ["2 × 4.187 × ln(317/353)","ΔS₁ = −0.9008 kJ/K"], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Cold water", ["3 × 4.187 × ln(317/293)","ΔS₂ = +0.9889 kJ/K"], ["k4","k4b"]], ["Total", ["−0.90076 + 0.98892","Sgen = +0.0882 kJ/K > 0"], ["k5","k5b"]]],live:[0,1,2],rh:80},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2 (total)",title:"Worked example 2: mixing water",say:'The cold water gains more: plus zero point nine eight eight nine. Nothing outside changes, so the total is the entropy generated: plus zero point zero eight eight two kilojoules per kelvin. Positive, so the mixing is irreversible, and possible.',pause:1.2,cues:{fk:'The cold water',k4:'gains more',k4b:'plus zero point nine',k5:'Nothing outside changes',k5b:'the entropy generated',a:'Positive, so the'},q:['Insulated vessel: 2 kg of water at 80 °C mixed with 3 kg at 20 °C.','cp = 4.187 kJ/kg·K. Find Tf and the total ΔS. Is the mixing reversible?'],f:['Tf = (m₁T₁ + m₂T₂) / (m₁ + m₂)','ΔS = m cp ln(Tf / T) for each mass','Sgen = ΔS₁ + ΔS₂ (insulated)'],hint:'Kelvin first. No heat or work crosses the vessel wall.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Kelvin", ["80 + 273 and 20 + 273","T₁ = 353 K, T₂ = 293 K"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Final temperature", ["(706 + 879) / 5 = 1585 / 5","Tf = 317 K = 44 °C"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Hot water", ["2 × 4.187 × ln(317/353)","ΔS₁ = −0.9008 kJ/K"], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Cold water", ["3 × 4.187 × ln(317/293)","ΔS₂ = +0.9889 kJ/K"], ["k4","k4b"]], ["Total", ["−0.90076 + 0.98892","Sgen = +0.0882 kJ/K > 0"], ["k5","k5b"]]],live:[3,4],rh:80,ans:['Tf = 317 K; Sgen = +0.0882 kJ/K: irreversible','a']},
{s:2,k:'trap2',term:'Exam trap',title:'Exam trap!',say:'Exam trap. Never say freezing water breaks the second law because its entropy falls. Write the full account: the surroundings gain more, and only the total must stay at or above zero.',cues:{x:'Exam trap',w:'Never say freezing',r:'Write the full'},wl:"Don't write",rl:'Write',wrong:['Water freezes, so ΔS of the water < 0:','the second law is broken'],right:['ΔS water + ΔS surroundings > 0:','only the total must be ≥ 0'],wv:(c,u,b)=>
 `<rect x="60" y="40" width="220" height="250" rx="12" fill="#EEF4FF" stroke="${TH_STEEL}" stroke-width="4"/>`+TH_T(170,78,'freezer',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TH_GREY})+
 `<rect x="120" y="140" width="100" height="100" rx="14" fill="#CFE8FF" stroke="${TH_COLD}" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M134,160 L160,186" stroke="#FFFFFF" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/>`+
 TH_arrow(330,90,330,230,TRED,6,22)+TH_T(360,140,'ΔS water < 0',{fs:28,fw:800,fill:TRED})+TH_T(360,196,'second law',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:TRED})+TH_T(360,230,'broken?',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:TRED}),
 rv:(c,u,b)=>{const z=190;
 return TH_L(50,z,590,z,TH_DARK,3)+`<rect x="80" y="${z}" width="110" height="60" rx="6" fill="${TH_COLD}"/>`+`<rect x="260" y="${z-110}" width="110" height="110" rx="6" fill="${ORG}"/>`+`<rect x="440" y="${z-50}" width="110" height="50" rx="6" fill="${GRN}"/>`+
  TH_T(135,z+90,'water',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TH_COLD})+TH_T(135,z+116,'falls',{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:TH_GREY})+
  TH_T(315,z-150,'surroundings',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:'#8A4B08'})+TH_T(315,z-124,'rise more',{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:TH_GREY})+
  TH_T(495,z-90,'total > 0',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})+TH_T(320,z+116,'(schematic)',{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:TH_GREY})}},
/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Entropy is a property, and under a reversible path on the T-s diagram, the area is heat. The total entropy of system and surroundings never falls. And your two numbers: one point three nine three, and plus zero point zero eight eight two.',cues:{r1:'Entropy is a',r2:'The total entropy',r3:'And your two'},panels:[["Entropy","BLU",'thTsI',['dS = δQrev / T: a property.','Area under a reversible path = heat.'],'r1'],["Principle of increase","ORG",'thAcct',['ΔS sys + ΔS surr = Sgen ≥ 0.','Zero only when reversible.'],'r2'],["The two key numbers","GRN",'thNums',['Same ΔS by either route.','Sgen > 0: the mixing is irreversible.'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is a reversible adiabatic process called isentropic?",say:'Question one. Why is a reversible adiabatic process called isentropic?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why is a'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is a reversible adiabatic process called isentropic?",say:'Adiabatic means delta Q is zero, and reversible means nothing is generated inside, so delta S is zero. Iso means same: the entropy stays the same.',cues:{a1:'Adiabatic means delta',a2:'Iso means same'},al:[["δQ = 0 and no internal irreversibility, so ΔS = ∫δQrev/T = 0.",'a1'],["Entropy stays constant: iso (same) + entropic.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{
 const b0=440;
 return TH_cyl(70,120,{w:150,h:170,ph:70,ins:1,gas:'#FFE8CC'})+TH_T(145,350,'insulated: Q = 0',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TH_WARM})+TH_T(145,382,'no friction',{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:TH_GREY})+
  g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),TH_ax(330,b0,260,380)+TH_L(450,110,450,370,GRN,7)+`<circle cx="450" cy="110" r="9" fill="${TH_DARK}"/><circle cx="450" cy="370" r="9" fill="${TH_DARK}"/>`+TH_T(468,118,'1',{fs:26,fw:800})+TH_T(468,378,'2',{fs:26,fw:800}))+
  g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),TH_T(470,250,'ΔS = 0',{fs:28,fw:800,fill:GRN})+TH_T(470,284,'same s',{fs:22,fill:TH_GREY}))}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"1 kg of air is heated at constant volume from 300 K to 600 K. Take cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K. Find ΔS.",say:'Question two. One kilogram of air is heated at constant volume, from three hundred to six hundred kelvin. Take c v as zero point seven one eight. Find delta S.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'One kilogram of'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"1 kg of air is heated at constant volume from 300 K to 600 K. Take cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K. Find ΔS.",say:'Constant volume, so use c v. The ratio is two again, and the log is zero point six nine three one. One times zero point seven one eight times that: zero point four nine eight kilojoules per kelvin.',cues:{fk:'Constant volume, so',k1:'The ratio is',k1b:'and the log',k2:'One times zero',k2b:'zero point four nine'},calc:{f:['v const: ΔS = m cv ln(T₂/T₁)'],hint:'Constant volume: use cv, not cp.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Temperature ratio",["T₂/T₁ = 600/300 = 2","ln 2 = 0.6931"],["k1","k1b"]],["Entropy change",["1 × 0.718 × 0.6931","ΔS = 0.498 kJ/K"],["k2","k2b"]]],live:[0,1]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"On a T-s diagram, why is a constant-volume line steeper than a constant-pressure line?",say:'Question three. On the T-s diagram, why is a constant-volume line steeper than a constant-pressure line?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'On the T-s'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"On a T-s diagram, why is a constant-volume line steeper than a constant-pressure line?",say:'At constant volume the slope is T over c v; at constant pressure it is T over c p. Since c v is smaller than c p, T over c v is larger, so the constant-volume line is steeper.',cues:{a1:'At constant volume',a2:'Since c v',a3:'so the constant-volume'},al:[["Slopes: dT/ds = T/cv at constant v, T/cp at constant p.",'a1'],["As cv < cp, T/cv is the larger slope.",'a2'],["So the constant-volume line is steeper.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{
 /* per kg of air from 300 K, computed: T = 300 exp(s/cv) and T = 300 exp(s/cp) */
 const x0=70,y0=450,sx=s=>x0+s/0.8*500,ty=T=>y0-(T-250)/400*380;
 const V=TH_range(0,TH_SV,30).map(s=>[sx(s),ty(TH_air(0.718,s))]),Pp=TH_range(0,TH_SP,30).map(s=>[sx(s),ty(TH_air(1.005,s))]);
 return TH_ax(x0,y0,540,400,'s, kJ/(kg·K)','T, K')+TH_T(x0-8,ty(300)+8,'300',{fs:22,a:'end',fill:TH_GREY})+TH_T(x0-8,ty(600)+8,'600',{fs:22,a:'end',fill:TH_GREY})+TH_L(x0,ty(600),sx(0.75),ty(600),'#CED4DA',2,'stroke-dasharray="6 6"')+
  `<circle cx="${sx(0)}" cy="${ty(300)}" r="8" fill="${TH_DARK}"/>`+
  g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),TH_poly(V,ORG,6)+TH_poly(Pp,BLU,6)+TH_T(sx(TH_SV)-10,ty(600)-16,'v const',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'end',fill:ORG})+TH_T(sx(TH_SP)+6,ty(600)-16,'p const',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU})+
   TH_T(sx(0.42),ty(330),'slope T/cp',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:BLU})+TH_T(sx(0.06),ty(470),'slope T/cv',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:ORG}))+
  g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),TH_T(sx(0.42),ty(380),'cv < cp: v line steeper',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:TH_DARK}))}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the two routes on the T-s diagram and the mixing chart, then check your numbers.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the two'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The two chapters", "Entropy: concept, T-s diagram and entropy change; Principle of increase of entropy"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["Two routes on the T-s diagram", "Air heated from 300 K to 600 K at constant pressure (solid) or at constant volume then isothermally (dashed): both end 0.697 kJ/(kg·K) higher; the shaded area is the heat."], ["The entropy account of mixing", "2 kg at 353 K and 3 kg at 293 K settle at 317 K: the hot water loses 0.9008 kJ/K, the cold gains 0.9889 kJ/K, and Sgen = +0.0882 kJ/K."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: P. K. Nag (entropy and Carnot-cycle chapters); Michael J. Moran (entropy and availability chapters); Yunus A. Çengel (entropy and Carnot-cycle chapters)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: entropy is a property, area is heat, and the total never falls. Next time, entropy generation and second-law analysis of control volumes. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'entropy is a',w2:'area is heat',w3:'and the total',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['ENTROPY IS A PROPERTY','BLU','w1'],['AREA = HEAT','ORG','w2'],['THE TOTAL NEVER FALLS','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Entropy generation and second-law analysis of control volumes", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
